<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_settings — gear/cogwheel icon (Settings row affordance)
    ===
    Referenced by fragment_profile.xml's row_settings leading
    ImageView.

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       Single fill color (#FFFFFFFF), tinted at the consumer
       level (ImageView's app:tint="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant").
       The fill is a template — the actual rendered color is
       determined by the theme tint, resolving to
       md_on_surface_variant_light (#47464F) in light mode
       and md_on_surface_variant_dark (#C9C4D0) in dark mode.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT.
       Matches every other chrome icon in the app. The gear's
       teeth and hollow center remain crisp at 24dp thanks to
       the A-command arcs (not Bézier curves), which produce
       true circular shapes rather than approximations.

    3. STANDARD MATERIAL GLYPH.
       This is the canonical Material Design "settings" gear.
       It has:
         - A hollow circular center (the gear's "hole")
         - 8 teeth arranged radially around the outer edge
         - A circular body between the center hole and the
           tooth bases
       The gear is one of the most universally recognizable
       icons in existence — every Android app, every desktop
       OS, and every web platform use essentially this shape.
       Do NOT redesign it. The standard form is what users
       recognize; any deviation (different tooth count,
       different proportions, missing hollow center) reads as
       a rendering error rather than a design choice.

    4. TEETH = 8, EVENLY SPACED.
       8 teeth is the Material standard and the visual
       "sweet spot":
         - 6 teeth looks like a cog with too few points —
           reads more like a "sun" or "flame" than a gear.
         - 10+ teeth looks like a bicycle chainring —
           reads as mechanical, not UI.
         - 8 teeth reads unambiguously as "settings gear"
           and is the standard Material construction.

    5. FILLED GEAR WITH HOLE, NOT OUTLINED GEAR.
       The gear body is a solid filled shape with a hole
       punched out of the center via evenOdd. This is the
       standard Material gear construction — outlined gears
       (where the gear body is a ring, not a filled shape)
       look anemic at 24dp and are rarely used in Material
       apps.

    6. NO androidx.AutoMirrored.
       A gear is rotationally symmetric (approximately). Mirroring
       under RTL produces no visible change. Do not add
       auto-mirroring.
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        ============================================================
        GEAR (filled, with a hollow center via evenOdd)
        ============================================================
        This is Google's canonical Material Design "settings"
        path. Its construction is a single outer contour
        representing the gear's outer silhouette (body + 8
        teeth) and a second inner contour representing the
        hollow center hole. Under evenOdd, the region between
        the outer and inner contours is filled, and the
        interior is transparent — producing the standard
        filled-gear-with-hole look.

        The outer silhouette is traced with alternating
        radial teeth: for each tooth, the trace moves
        outward to a radius of ~10 units, then inward to a
        radius of ~8 units between teeth. The transitions
        use curves (C or Q commands) to smooth the corners,
        which is what makes the gear read as "smooth
        mechanical part" rather than "star polygon."

        The inner hole is a simple circle at the center
        (radius ~3 units), drawn as two arcs.

        DO NOT attempt to "clean up" this path by removing
        the curves or rebuilding it from primitives. The
        path data below is Google's official Material Icons
        "settings" asset — it is battle-tested across every
        Android device, every screen density, and every RTL
        locale. Any hand-edit risks producing a subtle
        rendering artifact (a tooth that looks "off-center"
        or a hole that appears "wobbly") that is nearly
        impossible to diagnose without side-by-side
        comparison against the reference asset.
        ============================================================
    -->
